<DocsLayout active="production" title="Production configuration" description="The deployment-time decisions that turn the Redax application into an operated production boundary.">
  <section class="rdx-docs-callout"><b>Configure the environment around the service.</b><p>Redax does not select your cloud provider, region, Redis plan, telemetry vendor, DNS zone, or SLO for you. Those are deployment inputs. This page names what must be chosen, why it matters, and which Redax settings carry the choice into the runtime.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Hosting target and region</h2><p><b>What:</b> run the API and worker on one machine, private containers, or the checked-in Kubernetes reference topology. <b>Why:</b> the region determines network latency to your application/model provider and where request-derived operational data is stored. <b>How:</b> choose the target in your platform deployment configuration; Redax receives the resulting host, port, trusted-host, and Redis/telemetry endpoints as environment variables.</p><div class="rdx-docs-card-grid"><div><b>Local / Docker Desktop</b><span>Use Compose for development and offline-style local operation. No public DNS, cloud account, or managed Redis is required.</span></div><div><b>Private container host</b><span>Run API and worker as separate processes behind a private proxy. Mount durable audit storage and provide durable Redis.</span></div><div><b>Kubernetes</b><span>Use <code>deploy/kubernetes/</code> for replicas, probes, PDBs, rollout budgets, autoscaling, and network policies. Supply the external Redis Secret and ingress.</span></div><div><b>Cloud provider</b><span>No provider-specific Terraform or IAM module is shipped. Use your platform’s region, workload identity, secret, ingress, and managed Redis primitives.</span></div></div></section>
  <section><h2>Redis, HA, and backups</h2><p><b>What:</b> Redis backs rate limiting, response cache, idempotency leases, durable jobs, worker coordination, and optional centralized audit events. It is optional only when those features are disabled; the standard and scaled paths depend on it.</p><p><b>Production expectation:</b> use a TLS endpoint, authentication, private network access, replication/automatic failover, persistence appropriate to the job durability you promise, scheduled backups, and a tested restore. Set <code>REDAX_REDIS_URL</code>, <code>REDAX_REDIS_NAMESPACE</code>, <code>REDAX_REDIS_REQUIRED=true</code>, bounded pool/timeouts, and the Redis audit settings. A Redis outage makes readiness fail and rejects new durable jobs; cache-only failures may recompute.</p><div class="rdx-docs-body-table"><div><b>Development</b><span>Compose Redis, local volume, disposable credentials, no HA claim.</span></div><div><b>Production</b><span>Private TLS Redis, unique namespace, replica/failover, backups, restore drill, RPO/RTO owner.</span></div></div></section>
  <section><h2>Telemetry destination</h2><p>Redax emits structured stdout logs, Prometheus metrics at <code>/metrics</code>, and optional OpenTelemetry traces through <code>REDAX_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code>. Send each signal to an approved internal destination, define retention, and keep raw request values out of labels and attributes. To disable traces, leave the OTLP endpoint empty; keep metrics and error logs available for the SLOs you operate.</p><div class="rdx-docs-card-grid"><div><b>Logs</b><span>Collect container stdout with your platform shipper. Do not promote request text, API keys, or entity values to labels.</span></div><div><b>Metrics</b><span>Scrape <code>/metrics</code> from the private network. Alert on readiness, errors, latency, queue age, Redis pool, audit, and worker saturation.</span></div><div><b>Traces</b><span>Set <code>REDAX_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> to an OTLP gRPC collector. Validate metadata-only spans and exporter failure behavior.</span></div><div><b>Privacy</b><span>Retention, access control, export failure policy, and collector-side redaction belong to the operator.</span></div></div></section>
  <section><h2>Secrets, identity, and rotation</h2><p>Store secrets in the platform secret manager or an equivalent protected injection mechanism. Required production values include <code>REDAX_API_KEYS</code>, <code>REDAX_HASH_SALT</code>, <code>REDAX_JOB_PAYLOAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>, trusted hosts, and an audit integrity key when using the Redis audit backend. API keys can overlap during rotation: add the replacement, deploy, verify it, then revoke the old key with <code>REDAX_API_KEY_REVOCATIONS</code>. Treat hash salt, encryption keys, and audit keys as deployment-scoped credentials; rotation may require coordinated redeployment and can invalidate correlation or encrypted queued payloads depending on the key.</p><p>Redax does not ship a cloud IAM/control-plane integration. Use your platform’s workload identity for container-to-service access where available, and keep human/API-client identity at the API-key boundary.</p></section>
  <section><h2>DNS, TLS, and routing</h2><p>Terminate HTTPS at your reverse proxy or ingress, point a DNS record at that endpoint, and set <code>REDAX_TRUSTED_HOSTS</code> to the accepted hostname. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS at the proxy. Forward request bodies within <code>REDAX_MAX_BODY_BYTES</code>, preserve SSE streaming for <code>/v1/redact/stream</code>, and allow the configured stream timeout. Local Compose intentionally uses HTTP on localhost; it is not a public TLS configuration.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Recommended production SLOs</h2><p>These are starting targets, not guarantees. Measure them against your workload and record the chosen values with the deployment.</p><div class="rdx-docs-body-table"><div><b>Availability</b><span>Recommended starting target: 99.9% successful readiness/serving time, excluding planned maintenance.</span></div><div><b>Latency</b><span>Set separate p95/p99 targets for regex, model, batch, stream, and jobs; do not reuse the browser demo timing.</span></div><div><b>Errors</b><span>Alert on 5xx/503/504 rate, rejected overload, audit failures, Redis failures, and raw-safe-boundary violations.</span></div><div><b>Recovery</b><span>Define Redis RPO/RTO, worker retry/DLQ handling, rollout drain deadline, and backup restore verification.</span></div></div><p>Use <a href="/redax/docs/launch">the production launch gate</a> to run synthetic canaries and failure drills before exposing real traffic.</p></section>
</DocsLayout>
